're very good at  poker  uh have played professionally or online  um in kind of a full-time basis  and i was shocked when i heard that's  what they were all doing  and basically they're like look all the  money to be made is to basically find  these big fish that want to play private  games  right and they said because they got a  lot of money and they're not very good  at poker  and so you know there's a combination  it's a really good comic  only only if you're the guy who's good  at poker right  andy before i let you go ask everyone  the same two questions and then you'll  get to ask me one to uh to end it  but the first question is just what is  the most important book that you've ever  read  the one that changed my perspective  on technology was the  innovator's dilemma  theory from clay christensen is worthy  of a nobel prize  explain a little bit more why that was  so foundational or  kind of mind-changing for you  well disruption is perhaps the most  commonly misused term in technology  so clay christensen who passed away last  year  was a professor at harvard business  school and  he coined the term disruption  theory and it it had a precise  definition  unfortunately reporters and others have  come  to represent uh disruption as anything  that's new or different  but what clay found from his research  was that uh  if a company uh can come along  and deliver something that's simpler  cheaper  and more convenient than the incumbents  such that it is uneconomic for the  incumbent  to address it's an almost  impossible advantage to overcome  so there and there are two forms of of  disruption  new market disruption and low end  disruption so an example of new market  disruption  is where you serve non-consumers the  people  cannot consume the incumbent's  services so think sotheby's and ebay you  couldn't sell  you know your leftover items from a  garage sale at sotheby's because  they only sold things for 30 000 or more  now ebay was able to build a profitable  business  facilitating the sale of 20 items  now over time they were able to move up  market  and because of the breadth of their  market  they really ate into the profitability  of all other  retailers so that's an example of a new  market disruption  now it's called an innovative and then a  low-end disruption  is when you serve the over-served  in every market case people start adding  more and more features to the point that  people can't use them all you know think  about office phones  you know they're all these features on  an office phone that you've never used  so there's an opportunity to come up  with something simpler cheaper and more  convenient  a great example of that was dell with  versus compaq  when dell got started compact sold  computers  to resellers who then sold it to  companies and individuals  compact had a 40 gross margin  and the retailer had a 20 gross margin  you know resell margin  dell sold direct to consumers at a 25  gross margin now their cost of goods  sold was higher than compaq  which was a multi-billion dollar company  but not that much higher  so dell was selling computers directly  to individuals at a price  lower than what the retailer was paying  for compact  now compact could not so the problem  with a disruption  is it's uneconomic for the incumbent to  address  so if compaq tried to sell direct  they would have pissed off their channel  and lost their channel  now as a company with five billion  dollars of revenue and publicly traded  that would have destroyed their stock  price so it's called an innovator's  dilemma because you're damned if you do  and you're damned if you don't  if you ignore the innovator they move up  market like ebay did  and dell did and if you go after them  you kill your economics  so you're screwed either way and so a  truly disruptive company  that's able to deliver something simpler  cheaper more convenient  either to the overserved or  non-consumers  is almost impossible to stop  that's what